<header class="ws-head"><p class="ws-kicker">Worksheet &middot; Songs in History</p><h1 class="ws-title">Worksheet: Songs of the nineteenth century</h1><p class="ws-lede">A worksheet on 57 songs of the nineteenth century in Songs in History: when and where they were made, what they say and how they sound.</p><p class="ws-who" aria-hidden="true"><span>Name</span><span>Class</span><span>Date</span></p><p class="ws-tools"><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="0">Print the worksheet</button><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="1">Print with the answers</button><a href="/songs/?e=nineteenth">Open the songs &rarr;</a></p></header>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the songs in order</h2><p class="ws-how">Number the songs from 1 (the oldest) to 6 (the newest).</p><ul class="ws-order"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Scarborough Fair</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Deutschlandlied</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>God Save the Tsar!</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Silent Night</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Wiegenlied (Brahms's Lullaby)</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Mer Hayrenik (Our Fatherland)</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Where was each song made?</h2><p class="ws-how">Write the number of the right place next to each song. Their pages will help.</p><div class="ws-match"><ol class="ws-ma" type="A"><li>The Red Flag</li><li>Die Wacht am Rhein</li><li>Wiegenlied (Brahms's Lullaby)</li><li>Himno de Riego</li><li>O Holy Night</li><li>Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika</li></ol><ol class="ws-mb"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>Roquemaure, France</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>Switzerland (the poem); Krefeld, Prussia (Karl Wilhelm's music)</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>a Methodist mission school near Johannesburg, South Africa</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>Algeciras, Spain</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>not known</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>not known (written in Nicholas Donovan's house)</li></ol></div></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Think and discuss</h2><ol class="ws-qs"><li><p>Read the words of <b>Silent Night</b> on its page (in English if they are in another language). What is the song about, and who might have sung it?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Read the words of <b>Oh Shenandoah</b> on its page (in English if they are in another language). What is the song about, and who might have sung it?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Choose one song and listen to its performance. Which instruments or voices do you hear, and how does the music fit the words?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Songs travel: find a song on these pages that was sung far from where it was made, or that gave its tune to another song. How did it travel?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li></ol></section>
<p class="ws-src">From The Epoch Institute: the-epoch-institute.org/songs/?e=nineteenth. Free to print and copy for use in class.</p>
<details class="ws-key" id="answers"><summary>Answer key</summary><div class="ws-key-b"><p class="ws-key-t">Answer key: Worksheet: Songs of the nineteenth century</p><section><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the songs in order</h2><ol class="ws-key-list"><li>Silent Night <span>1818</span></li><li>God Save the Tsar! <span>1833</span></li><li>Deutschlandlied <span>1841</span></li><li>Scarborough Fair <span>c. 1850</span></li><li>Mer Hayrenik (Our Fatherland) <span>c. 1859</span></li><li>Wiegenlied (Brahms's Lullaby) <span>1868</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Where was each song made?</h2><ol class="ws-key-list" type="A"><li>The Red Flag <span>6: not known (written in Nicholas Donovan's house)</span></li><li>Die Wacht am Rhein <span>2: Switzerland (the poem); Krefeld, Prussia (Karl Wilhelm's music)</span></li><li>Wiegenlied (Brahms's Lullaby) <span>5: not known</span></li><li>Himno de Riego <span>4: Algeciras, Spain</span></li><li>O Holy Night <span>1: Roquemaure, France</span></li><li>Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika <span>3: a Methodist mission school near Johannesburg, South Africa</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Think and discuss</h2><p class="ws-key-note">Open questions: answers will vary. Good answers quote the song pages.</p></section></div></details>
